- How would you implement dynamic hot-swapping of strategies without downtime?
- How would you measure fairness and starvation in a multi-tenant system?

## Why This Is Asked
The question probes ability to design a dynamic, runtime-extensible cross-cutting concerns system using proxies, with per-method interceptor ordering and async support. It tests API design, runtime extensibility, and trade-offs among common approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Proxy-based interception for runtime extensibility
- Interceptor registry and per-method order
- Async support via promises and proper error propagation
- Trade-offs: Proxy vs Decorator vs Interceptor-chain

## Code Example
```javascript
type Context = { target: any; method: string; args: any[] };
interface Interceptor {
  name: string;
  intercept(ctx: Context, next: () => Promise<any>): Promise<any>;
}
class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, () => Interceptor>();
  register(name: string, factory: () => Interceptor) { this.map.set(name, factory); }
  createChain(names: string[]): Interceptor[] { return names.map(n => this.map.get(n)!).filter(Boolean); }
}
function createProxy<T>(target: T, registry: Registry, perMethod: Map<string, string[]>): T {
  return new Proxy(target as any, {
    get(obj, prop: string) {
      const orig = (obj as any)[prop];
      if (typeof orig !== 'function') return orig;
      return (...args: any[]) => {
        const order = perMethod.get(prop) || [];
        const chain = registry.createChain(order);
        let idx = -1;
        const next = (): Promise<any> => {
          idx++;
          if (idx >= chain.length) return Promise.resolve(orig.apply(obj, args));
          const interceptor = chain[idx];
          return interceptor.intercept({ target: obj, method: prop, args }, next);
        };
        return next();
      };
    }
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How handle error propagation and cancellation in the chain?
- How to test per-method interceptor ordering and runtime registration?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a runtime-extensible plugin architecture for critical control logic. It tests understanding of extensibility patterns, safety considerations, and observability requirements under high-load production environments similar to those at Netflix, Tesla, or Oracle.

## Key Concepts
- **Strategy Pattern**: Encapsulates each evaluation algorithm (Boolean, Percentage, UserSegment) behind a common interface
- **Registry/Plugin Loader**: Maintains a map of evaluator names to implementations, enabling runtime registration without redeployment
- **Hot-Reloadable Modules**: Allows new evaluators to be added or updated without restarting the service
- **Per-Flag Isolation**: Each flag has dedicated configuration and metrics to prevent cascade failures
- **Observability**: Built-in logging, metrics, and audit trails for every flag evaluation

## Code Example
```typescript
interface FlagEvaluationContext {
  userId?: string;
  attributes?: Record<string, any>;
  [key: string]: any;
}

interface FlagConfig {
  key: string;
  strategy: string;
  parameters?: Record<string, any>;
  enabled?: boolean;
}

interface IFlagEvaluator {
  name: string;
  evaluate(config: FlagConfig, context: FlagEvaluationContext): boolean;
}

class FlagRegistry {
  private evaluators = new Map<string, IFlagEvaluator>();
  private cache = new Map<string, IFlagEvaluator>();

  register(evaluator: IFlagEvaluator): void {
    this.evaluators.set(evaluator.name, evaluator);
    this.cache.delete(evaluator.name);
  }

  get(name: string): IFlagEvaluator | undefined {
    if (!this.cache.has(name)) {
      this.cache.set(name, this.evaluators.get(name));
    }
    return this.cache.get(name);
  }

  evaluate(config: FlagConfig, ctx: FlagEvaluationContext): boolean {
    const evaluator = this.get(config.strategy);
    if (!evaluator) throw new Error(`Unknown strategy: ${config.strategy}`);
    return evaluator.evaluate(config, ctx);
  }
}

// Built-in evaluators
const BooleanEvaluator: IFlagEvaluator = {
  name: 'Boolean',
  evaluate: (config) => config.parameters?.value === true,
};

const PercentageEvaluator: IFlagEvaluator = {
  name: 'Percentage',
  evaluate: (config, ctx) => {
    const percentage = config.parameters?.percentage ?? 0;
    const hash = this.hash(ctx.userId ?? Math.random().toString());
    return (hash % 100) < percentage;
  },
  hash: (str: string) => str.split('').reduce((a, b) => ((a << 5) - a) + b.charCodeAt(0), 0),
};

// Usage
const registry = new FlagRegistry();
registry.register(BooleanEvaluator);
registry.register(PercentageEvaluator);

const result = registry.evaluate(
  { key: 'new-feature', strategy: 'Percentage', parameters: { percentage: 25 } },
  { userId: 'user-123' }
);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests runtime extensibility, deterministic composition, and thread-safety in a real fraud-detection context.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for individual rules
- Registry for dynamic, ordered plugins
- Thread-safety and immutability for runtime changes
- Deterministic evaluation order

## Code Example
```javascript
export interface FraudRule {
  priority: number
  compute(transaction: Transaction): number
}

export class RuleRegistry {
  private rules: FraudRule[] = []
  register(r: FraudRule) { this.rules.push(r); this.rules.sort((a,b)=>a.priority-b.priority) }
  evaluate(tx: Transaction): number {
    let score = 0
    for (const r of this.rules) score += r.compute(tx)
    return score
  }
}
```

